File & Directory Commands
pwd         # Show current directory
ls          # List files
ls -l       # Detailed list
ls -a       # Show hidden files

cd folder   # Change directory
cd ..       # Move up one directory
cd ~        # Go to home directory

mkdir test  # Create directory
rmdir test  # Remove empty directory

touch file.txt   # Create empty file
cp a.txt b.txt   # Copy file
mv a.txt b.txt   # Rename/move file
rm file.txt      # Delete file
rm -r folder     # Delete folder recursively
Viewing File Contents
cat file.txt     # Display file content
less file.txt    # View file page by page
head file.txt    # First 10 lines
tail file.txt    # Last 10 lines
tail -f log.txt  # Live log monitoring
Search Commands
find . -name "file.txt"   # Find file
grep "word" file.txt      # Search text in file
grep -r "word" .          # Recursive search
Permissions
chmod 755 script.sh   # Change permissions
chown user:file a.txt # Change ownership

Permission meanings:

r = read
w = write
x = execute
Process Management
ps aux        # List processes
top           # Live process monitor
kill PID      # Kill process
kill -9 PID   # Force kill
System Information
uname -a      # System info
df -h         # Disk usage
du -sh folder # Folder size
free -h       # Memory usage
uptime        # System uptime
Networking
ping google.com      # Check connectivity
ifconfig             # Network info (older)
ip addr              # Network info (modern)
curl example.com     # Fetch webpage
wget URL             # Download file
Package Management
Ubuntu/Debian
sudo apt update
sudo apt install nginx
CentOS/RHEL
sudo yum install nginx
Compression
tar -cvf files.tar folder/
tar -xvf files.tar

zip -r files.zip folder/
unzip files.zip
User Management
whoami          # Current user
adduser john    # Add user
passwd john     # Change password
Useful Shortcuts
clear        # Clear terminal
history      # Command history
man ls       # Manual for command
Ctrl + C     # Stop running command
Ctrl + R     # Search history
Beginner Tip

Use:

man <command>

Example:

man grep

to learn detailed usage for any command.